# FUD Court web — PUBLIC listener (TLS) for apps/web (C2).
#
# WHAT THIS IS
# ------------
# `fc.dwirijal.my.id` is a PUBLIC hostname, not an internal one. Caddy already
# terminates TLS for public names on this host (that is why `caddy.service` is
# up and binds :443 — `default_bind` in /etc/caddy/Caddyfile only constrains the
# other site, it does not apply here). So the front for this host terminates
# TLS AND compresses in one listener, and the Cloudflare tunnel is REMOVED from
# the path for this hostname.
#
# WHY NOT `http://` THROUGH THE TUNNEL
# ------------------------------------
# A bare `http://` site on any other port answers correctly but does NOT run a
# content-encoding encoder (verified on this host 2026-10-09: the same config on
# :3511 returned `Content-Encoding: gzip` for `/api/ticker`; the encoder is
# installed by the HTTP-protocol/tls certificate app, not by `encode` alone).
# Going straight to TLS:443 is therefore both the fewer-hops option and the one
# that compresses.
#
# WHAT CHANGES OUTSIDE THIS REPO (host-level files, done at deploy time)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#   1. sudo cp deploy/caddy/fudcourt-web-internal.caddy /etc/caddy/   ← optional internal listener
#      sudo cp deploy/caddy/fudcourt-web-public.caddy  /etc/caddy/fudcourt-web-public.caddy
#   2. echo 'import /etc/caddy/fudcourt-web-public.caddy' >> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#      (an `import` keeps this repo file authoritative for this hostname and
#       leaves the existing :80 handlers untouched)
#   3. sudo caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile && sudo systemctl reload caddy
#   4. REMOVE the fc.dwirijal.my.id ingress from the tunnel:
#        ~/.cloudflared/config.yml — delete:
#          - hostname: fc.dwirijal.my.id
#            service: http://127.0.0.1:3100
#      then: systemctl restart cloudflared
#   5. FLIP THE DNS RECORD from the tunnel (proxied CNAME to
#      <tunnel>.cfargotunnel.com) to the ORIGIN A/AAAA for fc.dwirijal.my.id.
#      Until step 5 the hostname still resolves to the tunnel and this listener
#      is never reached, so the change is inert until you intend it.
#
# ROLLBACK
# --------
# Restore the two lines in ~/.cloudflared/config.yml, restore the DNS record to
# the tunnel CNAME, `systemctl restart cloudflared`. The app was never changed,
# so nothing else has to move.
#
# CAVEAT, STATED HONESTLY
# -----------------------
#  * rsautl/ACME: this listener must obtain the certificate itself (Let's
#    Encrypt via HTTP-01 or TLS-ALPN). Cloudflare's "Full" mode is fine with a
#    publicly-trusted cert; "Flexible" would loop, so leave SSL/TLS mode at
#    Full (strict) or above when the DNS record is moved.
#  * Cloudflare's proxy (`proxied` record) would re-add its own CDN layer for
#    this host; that layers fine (it re-compresses/streams) but it re-introduces
#    the same edge masking this config exists to remove. If you want the
#    uncompressed-origin proof to hold in production, use a DNS-only (grey)
#    record for this hostname.
#  * Neither file is deployed by this run; this run only proves the compression
#    behaviour on the internal listener (:3110) against the real app.
#
{
	email admin@dwirijal.my.id
	log {
		output stderr
		format console
	}
}

fc.dwirijal.my.id {
	encode zstd gzip

	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3100 {
		flush_interval -1
	}
}
